<article>
<h1>Right vs Left Service in Restaurants: What’s the Difference?</h1>
<p class="byline">By <span>Nigel A. Thomas</span> • Hospitality Executive &amp; Corporate Trainer</p>
<section class="intro">
<h2>It’s Not Just Tradition — It’s Function</h2>
<p>Ask ten servers which side to serve from and you’ll often get ten different answers delivered with total confidence. The truth is that service side isn’t arbitrary — each style exists to keep the guest comfortable, the service fast, and the front-of-house team moving without collisions. Here’s how the main styles break down.</p>
</section>
<section class="breakdown">
<h2>Fine Dining Service</h2>
<ul>
<li>Serve from the LEFT</li>
<li>Clear from the RIGHT</li>
</ul>
<h2>Silver / French Service</h2>
<ul>
<li>Serve from the LEFT</li>
<li>French style service — plating tableside from a platter</li>
</ul>
<h2>Serve from the RIGHT</h2>
<ul>
<li>Soup and drinks</li>
<li>Pre-plated dishes</li>
</ul>
<h2>Guest Comfort Comes First</h2>
<p>Every rule here serves three goals: guest comfort, service speed, and staff attention. When in doubt, the side that avoids reaching across a guest or crossing another server’s path is almost always the right one.</p>
<h2>Most Used: Plate Service (American Service)</h2>
<p>In day-to-day restaurant operations, plate service dominates — food is fully plated in the kitchen and brought straight to the table. It’s fast, practical, and the standard for the vast majority of covers outside fine dining.</p>
</section>
<div class="quote-block">
      Fine dining is elegance and detail. Plate service is speed and efficiency. Not every restaurant needs complex service — but every restaurant needs smart, comfortable, and efficient service.
    </div>
<div class="ad-slot">
